  "worked_solution": "First, to find ${H}^{2},$ we multiply $H$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{2} & =H\\cdot H \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}23 & -18 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${H}^{3}={H}^{2}\\cdot H,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{3} & ={H}^{2}\\cdot H \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}23 & -18 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}97 & -118 \\\\ 59 & 38\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
